From: shouchen <TheOneovb@petlover.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: skip init commands the WCN3990 claims but rejects

The supported-commands bitmap returned by Read Local Supported Commands is
a hint, not a promise, and the WCN3990 on this board (soc 0x40010224, rom
0x1001 patched with qca/crbtfw21.tlv) advertises LE commands that answer
with "Unknown HCI Command" (0x01).

hci_init_stage_sync() treats any error from a stage as fatal, so the first
such lie aborts hci_init_sync() and leaves hci0 unconfigured - one quirk per
opcode does not scale here, they come one after another:

  hci0: HCI Read Transmit Power Level command is advertised, but not supported.
  hci0: Opcode 0x2031 failed: -56
  hciconfig hci0 up -> Can't init device hci0: Invalid request code (56)

Reported by bt_to_errno() as -EBADRQC, 0x01 means the controller does not
know the opcode at all: nothing was half-applied, nothing can be retried, so
skipping it is the only sensible interpretation.  Gate that on a quirk and
set the quirk for WCN3990 in the vendor setup callback.

--- a/include/net/bluetooth/hci.h
+++ b/include/net/bluetooth/hci.h
@@ -252,6 +252,28 @@
 	 */
 	HCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_READ_TRANSMIT_POWER,
 
+	/*
+	 * When this quirk is set, an "Unknown HCI Command" (0x01) status from
+	 * any command of an init stage is logged and skipped instead of
+	 * aborting the whole init sequence.
+	 *
+	 * The supported-commands bitmap is a hint, not a promise.  Some
+	 * controllers - the WCN3990 on raphael (rom 0x1001 patched with
+	 * crbtfw21.tlv) advertises LE commands it then rejects - abort init
+	 * on the first such lie and leave the controller dead:
+	 *
+	 *   hci0: Opcode 0x204b failed: -56
+	 *   hci0: Opcode 0x2031 failed: -56
+	 *
+	 * Ignoring the status is safe: 0x01 means the controller has no idea
+	 * what the opcode is, so there is nothing to retry and nothing was
+	 * half-applied.
+	 *
+	 * This quirk can be set before hci_register_dev is called or
+	 * during the hdev->setup vendor callback.
+	 */
+	HCI_QUIRK_TOLERATE_UNKNOWN_INIT_CMD,
+
 	/* When this quirk is set, HCI_OP_SET_EVENT_FLT requests with
 	 * HCI_FLT_CLEAR_ALL are ignored and event filtering is
 	 * completely avoided. A subset of the CSR controller
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
@@ -3773,6 +3773,20 @@
 		int err;
 
 		err = stage[i].func(hdev);
+
+		/* -EBADRQC is how bt_to_errno() reports "Unknown HCI Command"
+		 * (status 0x01).  For a controller that sets bits for opcodes
+		 * it does not implement, that is not a broken init sequence,
+		 * it is a command that does not apply here - the only thing
+		 * aborting on it achieves is leaving the controller down.
+		 */
+		if (err == -EBADRQC &&
+		    hci_test_quirk(hdev, HCI_QUIRK_TOLERATE_UNKNOWN_INIT_CMD)) {
+			bt_dev_info(hdev, "init stage %zu (%ps): unsupported, skipped",
+				    i, (void *)stage[i].func);
+			continue;
+		}
+
 		if (err)
 			return err;
 	}
--- a/drivers/bluetooth/btqca.c
+++ b/drivers/bluetooth/btqca.c
@@ -763,6 +763,15 @@
 
 	bt_dev_info(hdev, "QCA controller version 0x%08x", soc_ver);
 
+	/*
+	 * This ROM's Read Local Supported Commands bitmap is not trustworthy:
+	 * it advertises LE commands (0x204b, 0x2031, ...) that come back as
+	 * "Unknown HCI Command".  Every one of them aborts hci_init_sync()
+	 * and leaves hci0 unconfigured, so let the init stages skip them.
+	 */
+	if (soc_type == QCA_WCN3990)
+		hci_set_quirk(hdev, HCI_QUIRK_TOLERATE_UNKNOWN_INIT_CMD);
+
 	config.user_baud_rate = baudrate;
 
 	/* Firmware files to download are based on ROM version.
